// ADR-149: a chunking partitions and a window slides, and both answer a
// sequence of sequences.
//
// Every line below was `Y110` — "no method `chunks` on type `Vec[Int]`" — until
// the descriptor question the deferral note in `builtins.rs` recorded was
// answered: the outer `Vec` is labelled `VEC` and each inner one carries the
// receiver's own element descriptor, at every length including zero.
//
// The two are barriers like `sorted` and `reversed`, so a chain ends at one and
// starts again from its result. What they are *for* is the shape at the bottom
// of this program: "compare each element with its neighbour" and "take these in
// runs of three" are two of the most common things a puzzle asks, and neither
// had a spelling that was not an index and a bound.

var v = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

// A chunking is a partition: every element once, in order, and a length the
// size does not divide leaves a **short last chunk** rather than dropping
// the tail or padding it.
out(v.chunks(2))
out(v.chunks(5))
// Wider than the sequence is one short chunk, not a fault and not one chunk
// per element.
out(v.chunks(9))

// A window slides by one and keeps only the runs that **fit**, which is the
// one place the two differ. `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5].windows(9)` is `[]`: "which runs
// of nine are there" is a good question about a sequence of five, and its
// answer is none.
out(v.windows(2))
out(v.windows(5))
out(v.windows(9))

// Not shown because it exits 1: `v.chunks(0)` and `v.windows(0 - 1)` fault
// with `size or extent out of range`. A run of zero elements is not a short
// run — chunking a non-empty sequence into them has no finite answer — and a
// negative one names nothing. That is the whole of what either refuses.

// An empty receiver is an empty answer at any size, and the empty answer is
// still a `Vec[Vec[Int]]` rather than an unlabelled `Vec` — which is the
// decision, and is invisible until something reads the label.
var none = Vec[Int]()
out(none.chunks(2))
out(none.chunks(2).count())

// The generic receiver, so the same two rows serve every iterable a `for`
// walks (ADR-127 decision 1). A `Range` and a `Text` are walked in place; a
// `Set` and a `Map` are snapshotted through their own accessors first.
out((0..6).chunks(3))
out("abcd".windows(2))

var s = Set()
s.insert(3)
s.insert(1)
s.insert(2)
out(s.chunks(2))

var m = Map()
m["a"] = 1
m["b"] = 2
out(m.chunks(1))

// A barrier ends a fused chain and starts a new one, so a stage in front
// groups what **survived** and a stage behind consumes the groups — which
// only works because each group is a sequence in its own right.
out(v.filter(|n| n % 2 == 1).chunks(2))
out(v.windows(2).map(|w| w.sum()))
out(v.chunks(2).map(|c| c.count()).max())

// Neither reads a descriptor callback, so like `reversed` they carry no
// bound on the element: a `Vec` of closures groups where `sorted()` is
// `Y006`.
var fs = Vec()
fs.push(|x| x + 1)
fs.push(|x| x + 2)
fs.push(|x| x + 3)
out(fs.windows(2).count())

// Groups **share** their elements rather than copying them, which is the
// language's reference semantics and not a rule of these rows: the `2` in
// the first window and the `2` in the second are one object.
var a = Vec[Int]()
var b = Vec[Int]()
var outer = Vec()
outer.push(a)
outer.push(b)
var w = outer.windows(2)
w[0][1].push(9)
out(b)

// ...and what the two are for. Counting where a sequence increases used to
// be an index and a bound; it is a window and a predicate.
var depths = [199, 200, 208, 210, 200, 207, 240, 269, 260, 263]
out(depths.windows(2).count(|p| p[1] > p[0]))
// The same question over a sliding sum of three, which is the second half of
// that puzzle and the reason `windows` slides rather than partitions.
var sums = depths.windows(3).map(|t| t.sum())
out(sums.windows(2).count(|p| p[1] > p[0]))
// And the partitioning half: a batch at a time.
out(depths.chunks(4).map(|batch| batch.count()))
